> I think you overestimate the damage that upper dungeon monsters can inflict.

You should not _underestimate_ damage done by the monsters; especially
in the upper levels. (Says one who ascends on quite a regular basis if
not in too bad mood.) I think that's better advice. Too many characters
die on the upper levels, too many roles are wimpy enough in the beginning
that careful play is required, which means *not* to risk taking damage
from the "upper dungeon monsters".

>>As to loot, in the early game that's a matter of going into the
>>mines not exploring a level at a time.
> 
> Not true. I find that vast majority of the loot in the upper dungeon is
> in shops. No shops on level 1. So I get off that level as soon as it's
> fully explored no matter what XL I happen to be.

To tell the extreme I've had a lot games where no shop at all had been
generated in the main dungeon. Or only "useless" shops.

Primary source of loot was mostly objects carried by monsters and death
drops from monsters and loot lying around. But also in this case you have
to proceed to find the monsters and the loot lying around (as with shops).
A fitting shop may be very advantageous. And a random bones level may
occasionally provide helpful loot, too.

>> Consider that serial ascenders don't often play the protection racket.
> 
> Question: How do serial ascenders manage the AC/weight/spellcasting balance?

The whole game of Nethack (which is so well balanced) is a balance issue
also WRT the tactics; I am sure that serial ascenders balance all options
they have depending on the current role/race and the many random factors
of the game.

Weight? Drop loot, build stashes!
AC? Increase whenever possible (special case for Monks, Wizards, etc.)
Spellcasting? Enhance it. Decide in every game, in every new phase what
will help you most. There are situations where removing armor to cast
spells is feasable. Or prefer ranged attacks or use your attack spells;
don't let the monster come close.

There's *not* only one way (strategy) how to do "it". It always depends
on the circumstances.

